  "raw_text": "Step 1: We start by examining the given recurrence relation for \\( f(n+1) \\):\n\\[ f(n + 1) = (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2f(n). \\]\n\nStep 2: To find a pattern, we calculate the first few terms of the sequence using the initial condition \\( f(1) = f(1986) \\).\n\nStep 3: Calculate \\( f(2) \\):\n\\[ f(2) = (-1)^2 \\cdot 1 - 2f(1) = 1 - 2f(1). \\]\n\nStep 4: Calculate \\( f(3) \\):\n\\[ f(3) = (-1)^3 \\cdot 2 - 2f(2) = -2 - 2(1 - 2f(1)) = -2 - 2 + 4f(1) = -4 + 4f(1). \\]\n\nStep 5: Calculate \\( f(4) \\):\n\\[ f(4) = (-1)^4 \\cdot 3 - 2f(3) = 3 - 2(-4 + 4f(1)) = 3 + 8 - 8f(1) = 11 - 8f(1). \\]\n\nStep 6: Notice that the terms alternate in sign and involve multiples of \\( f(1) \\). Let's hypothesize a general form for \\( f(n) \\) and verify it.\n\nStep 7: Assume \\( f(n) = a_n f(1) + b_n \\), where \\( a_n \\) and \\( b_n \\) are sequences to be determined. From the recurrence relation, we can derive:\n\\[ f(n+1) = (-1)^{n+1} n - 2f(n). \\]\nSubstituting \\( f(n) = a_n f(1) + b_n \\) into the recurrence:\n\\[ a_{n+1} f(1) + b_{n+1} = (-1)^{n+1} n - 2(a_n f(1) + b_n). \\]\nThis gives us two equations:\n\\[ a_{n+1} f(1) = (-1)^{n+1} n - 2a_n f(1), \\]\n\\[ b_{n+1} = -2b_n. \\]\n\nStep 8: Solve for \\( b_n \\):\n\\[ b_{n+1} = -2b_n. \\]\nSince \\( b_1 = 0 \\) (from \\( f(1) = f(1986) \\)), all \\( b_n = 0 \\).\n\nStep 9: Solve for \\( a_n \\):\n\\[ a_{n+1} f(1) = (-1)^{n+1} n - 2a_n f(1), \\]\n\\[ a_{n+1} = (-1)^{n+1} \\frac{n}{f(1)} - 2a_n. \\]\n\nStep 10: We observe that the sum of the series \\( f(1) + f(2) + \\cdots + f(1985) \\) can be simplified by pairing terms. Notice that:\n\\[ f(2k-1) + f(2k) = (-1)^{2k-1} (2k-1) - 2f(2k-1) + (-1)^{2k} 2k - 2f(2k) = -(2k-1) + 2k - 2(f(2k-1) + f(2k)). \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ f(2k-1) + f(2k) = 1 - 2(f(2k-1) + f(2k)). \\]\nThus,\n\\[ 3(f(2k-1) + f(2k)) = 1, \\]\n\\[ f(2k-1) + f(2k) = \\frac{1}{3}. \\]\n\nStep 11: There are 992 such pairs from 1 to 1984, and the last term is \\( f(1985) \\):\n\\[ f(1) + f(2) + \\cdots + f(1985) = 992 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} + f(1985). \\]\n\nStep 12: Since \\( f(1985) = f(1) \\),\n\\[ f(1) + f(2) + \\cdots + f(1985) = 992 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} + f(1) = \\frac{992}{3} + f(1). \\]\n\nStep 13: Given \\( f(1) = f(1986) \\), and knowing the periodicity and symmetry, the sum simplifies to:\n\\[ f(1) + f(2) + \\cdots + f(1985) = 0. \\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{0}<|im_end|>",
